/-!
# Base change of the conormal algebra

Let

```
Z  --i-->  X
|          |
f          g
v          v
Z' --i'--> X'
```

be a cartesian square of schemes with `i` and `i'` immersions.  The canonical morphism
`f^* 𝒞_{Z'/X',*} ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X,*}` of graded `𝒪_Z`-algebras is then surjective in every degree, and
it is an isomorphism as soon as the ambient morphism `g` is flat.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.conormalPiece`: the degree-`n` homogeneous piece of the conormal algebra,
  as a sheaf of modules on the source.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.conormalHomSection`: the sectionwise canonical morphism of Tag 0634 over a
  pair of compatible opens.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.canonicalPushforwardHom`: the canonical morphism in each degree, as a
  morphism into the pushforward along `f`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.CanonicalPullbackHom`: the canonical morphism in each degree, presented as
  a map out of the pullback sheaf, together with the identification of its adjoint with the
  sectionwise morphism of the previous section.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.canonicalPullbackHom`: that presentation, for a commutative square.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.conormalAlgebraMap_epi`: **Stacks Tag 0635**, surjectivity in every
  degree.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.conormalAlgebraMap_isIso_of_flat`: **Stacks Tag 0635**, the flat
  base-change isomorphism in every degree.

## Implementation notes

Surjectivity is local, so both statements are proved on the affine charts of the square: a pair
of affine opens `Spec R = U ⊆ X` and `Spec R' = U' ⊆ X'` with `g(U) ⊆ U'` on which `i` and `i'`
restrict to closed immersions.  Over such a chart the square of rings of functions is a pushout
with `R' ⟶ Γ(Z', i'⁻¹U')` surjective, so the kernel ideal downstairs extends to the kernel
ideal upstairs, `I = I' R`.  Surjectivity of `⨁ₙ (I')ⁿ/(I')ⁿ⁺¹ ⟶ ⨁ₙ Iⁿ/Iⁿ⁺¹` then says that
`Iⁿ` is generated by the image of `(I')ⁿ`, which is immediate, and surjectivity of a morphism
of sheaves of modules follows because these charts form a basis of the topology of `Z`.

When `g` is flat the chart map `R' ⟶ R` is flat, and then `R ⊗_{R'} (I')ⁿ/(I')ⁿ⁺¹ ⟶ Iⁿ/Iⁿ⁺¹`
is bijective: surjectivity as above, and injectivity because `(I')ⁿ/(I')ⁿ⁺¹` injects into
`R'/(I')ⁿ⁺¹` and tensoring a flat extension preserves that.  Those chart bijections are glued
into an inverse of the morphism of sheaves, which is then an isomorphism because the morphism
is epi.

Two conventions keep the elaborator out of the quotients and tensor products that these proofs
are built from.  First, every purely ring-theoretic step is stated for an abstract ring map,
away from sheaves of modules; `mem_of_mem_map_pow` and `associatedGradedBaseChangeEquiv` are the
two statements that carry the whole local computation.  Second, sections of a sheaf of modules
are always named by `Γ(M, U)`, never by the underlying `ModuleCat` object, so the scalar action
of `Γ(X, U)` is the one found by instance search; the thin wrappers `conormalPiece`,
`conormalLocalMap`, `conormalHomSection` and `chartSectionsEquiv` move the data of the previous
sections into that spelling once and for all.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0635](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0635)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0634](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0634)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0633](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0633)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0630](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0630)
-/

